    "meal_prep": {
      "type": "image-with-text",
      "blocks": {
        "m_sub": {
          "type": "subheading",
          "settings": {
            "subheading": "Plan it ahead",
            "subheading_size": "subtitle-md",
            "secondary_color": true
          }
        },
        "m_head": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "The salt goes on hours before the pan",
            "heading_size": "title-md",
            "heading_tag": "h2",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "m_text": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>Salt the salmon in the morning or at lunch and leave it uncovered on a rack in the fridge &mdash; one to three hours is the window, and that is all the salt the fish gets. Don&rsquo;t take it overnight: fish is more delicate than steak and the texture goes firm. The snap pea pesto makes far more than two plates need and it keeps, so it is worth making a full batch. The sear is the one part that will not wait, so cook it last.</p>",
            "text_size": "subtext-md",
            "text_max_size": 32,
            "text_font": "body",
            "secondary_color": false,
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "m_btn": {
          "type": "button",
