    "faq_shrimp": {
      "type": "faq",
      "blocks": {
        "q_size": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Our King Salmon portions are 8.5 oz. Does the recipe still work?",
            "text": "<p>Yes. The recipe is written for 6 oz portions, and a larger portion is thicker, so it takes longer to come up to temperature. Keep the sear, the rest and the finish in the same order, and use the thermometer rather than the clock: pull at 130&deg;F in the thickest part. Scale the salt with the weight &mdash; about &frac34; tsp Diamond Crystal per 8.5 oz portion.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_rest": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Why take the fish out halfway through?",
            "text": "<p>Because the skin and the flesh want different things. The skin crisps in a few minutes; the middle of a thick fillet takes longer. Four minutes on a rack, skin up, lets the middle catch up without the skin sitting on heat. Take the pan off the burner too &mdash; an empty skillet left on the heat comes back hot enough to burn the skin you just built.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_temp": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Why pull it at 130&deg;F?",
            "text": "<p>It keeps cooking after it leaves the pan and finishes around 135&deg;F on the plate. Wait for 135&deg;F in the pan and it climbs past where you want it, which is the difference between silky and chalky. The 130&deg;F reading is taken at the very end, not during the rest.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_brine": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Do I have to dry-brine it?",
            "text": "<p>No, but it is worth it if you can. Salting one to three hours ahead, uncovered on a rack in the fridge, seasons the flesh and dries the skin so it crisps. If you are cooking right now, salt the fillets as they go into the pan instead. Don&rsquo;t go overnight &mdash; fish is more delicate than steak and the texture firms up.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_salt": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "I only have Morton kosher salt.",
            "text": "<p>Use half as much. Morton&rsquo;s crystals are denser, so a teaspoon of it is saltier than a teaspoon of Diamond Crystal, which is what the quantities here are written for.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_thaw": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How do I thaw the salmon?",
            "text": "<p>Overnight in the refrigerator in the sealed pack, about 12 hours, then pat the skin completely dry before it goes in the pan. In a hurry, submerge the sealed pack in cold water and change the water every 20 minutes. Never thaw fish on the counter or out of its pack &mdash; it takes on water, and wet skin will not crisp.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "q_size",
        "q_rest",
        "q_temp",
        "q_brine",
        "q_salt",
        "q_thaw"
      ],
